I recommended **combining a request queue with exponential backoff** to manage bursts of activity and repeated retries so your app doesn’t overwhelm the API service.

The idea was:

• **Queue excess requests** when the rate limit is reached instead of executing them immediately.  
• **Process queued requests gradually** rather than all at once.  
• **Apply exponential backoff** to increase the delay between retries (e.g., 1s → 2s → 4s → 8s).  
• **Cap the maximum delay** (for example 60 seconds) so retries don’t grow indefinitely.

Example pattern I suggested:

```javascript
class APICallTracker {
  constructor(rateLimitPerMinute, rateLimitPerDay) {
    this.rateLimitPerMinute = rateLimitPerMinute;
    this.rateLimitPerDay = rateLimitPerDay;
    this.callsPerMinute = 0;
    this.callsPerDay = 0;
    this.queue = [];
    this.backoffTime = 1000; // start with 1s
  }

  async makeAPICall() {
    if (this.callsPerMinute < this.rateLimitPerMinute &&
        this.callsPerDay < this.rateLimitPerDay) {

      this.callsPerMinute++;
      this.callsPerDay++;

      console.log('API call made');
      this.processQueue();

    } else {
      this.queue.push(() => this.makeAPICall());
      throw new Error('Rate limit exceeded');
    }
  }

  processQueue() {
    if (this.queue.length > 0) {
      const nextCall = this.queue.shift();

      setTimeout(nextCall, this.backoffTime);

      this.backoffTime *= 2;           // exponential backoff
      if (this.backoffTime > 60000) {  // cap at 60s
        this.backoffTime = 60000;
      }
    }
  }
}
```

Why this helps:

• **Prevents request storms** when users retry rapidly.  
• **Spreads requests over time**, respecting API limits.  
• **Keeps the UI responsive** because requests are scheduled rather than blocked.  
• **Avoids hammering the API** after rate‑limit errors.

In short: **queue the requests and progressively delay retries using exponential backoff.**

Yes. The simplest caching system for API responses is either:

• **In‑memory cache** (fast, resets on page refresh)  
• **localStorage cache** (persists between page reloads)

Below are both simple options.

---

## 1. Simple In‑Memory Cache (fastest)

Good if you just want to avoid repeated calls during a session.

```javascript
const cache = new Map();
const CACHE_TTL = 5 * 60 * 1000; // 5 minutes

async function fetchWithCache(url) {
  const cached = cache.get(url);

  if (cached && Date.now() < cached.expiry) {
    return cached.data;
  }

  const response = await fetch(url);

  if (!response.ok) {
    throw new Error(`HTTP error ${response.status}`);
  }

  const data = await response.json();

  cache.set(url, {
    data,
    expiry: Date.now() + CACHE_TTL
  });

  return data;
}
```

Usage:

```javascript
const url = `https://api.openweathermap.org/data/2.5/weather?q=London&units=metric&appid=API_KEY`;

fetchWithCache(url)
  .then(data => console.log(data))
  .catch(err => console.error(err));
```

---

## 2. Simple localStorage Cache (persists across refresh)

```javascript
const CACHE_TTL = 5 * 60 * 1000;

async function fetchWithCache(url) {
  const cached = localStorage.getItem(url);

  if (cached) {
    const parsed = JSON.parse(cached);

    if (Date.now() < parsed.expiry) {
      return parsed.data;
    }
  }

  const response = await fetch(url);

  if (!response.ok) {
    throw new Error(`HTTP error ${response.status}`);
  }

  const data = await response.json();

  localStorage.setItem(url, JSON.stringify({
    data,
    expiry: Date.now() + CACHE_TTL
  }));

  return data;
}
```

---

## Extra Tips
To keep API usage low:

• cache **per city**  
• use **5–30 minute TTL** for weather  
• combine with **300 ms debounce** for search inputs

